WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
 (upbeat music)

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.620
 (speaking in foreign language)

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:08.200
 (upbeat music)

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:14.640
 - Hello and welcome to another episode

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.840
 of "No Such Thing as a Fish," a weekly podcast

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.280
 this week coming to you live

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.880
 from the Royal Institution in London.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.880
 (audience cheering)

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.920
 My name is Dan Schreiber.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.720
 I am sitting here with Anna Toshinsky,

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.960
 Andrew Hunter Murray, and James Harkin.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.120
 And once again, we have gathered around the microphones

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.960
 with our four favorite facts from the last seven days

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.280
 and in a particular order, here we go.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.500
 Starting with fact number one, and that is Andy.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.080
 - Myfactiz, the first ever megawatt scale wind turbine

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.000
 was erected at a Vermont mountain called Grandpas Nob.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.240
 (audience laughing)

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.200
 - Okay, so this is wind turbine

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.320
 and it's a megawatt scale, you know,

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.640
 it could generate a megawatt of electricity at any one time.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.520
 That was the capacity.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.320
 We're in 1941, October 1941, just before Pearl Harbor,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.160
 coincidence.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.680
 - So America had nothing to do at that time again.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.520
 - I know, it was easy breezy times,

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.160
 but no, that's just a setting the scene for you.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.840
 And there was a guy called Palmer Putnam,

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.960
 who was both a sailor and an engineer.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.640
 He lived a very near Grandpas Nob.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.120
 He kept cod, and he had two problems.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.160
 One was constantly windy,

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.240
 just constantly the wind blowing in off the Atlantic,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.160
 just constantly, his electricity bills were too high.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.320
 And he thought, I wonder if I could combine

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.600
 these problems into one solution.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.680
 And he had a bit of expertise in the field,

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.120
 and he built this wind turbine.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.160
 It lasted about, not very long, sadly, about two years,

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.640
 but it was groundbreaking because there had been turbines

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.560
 before they could generate electricity,

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.320
 but this was the biggest that had ever been built,

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.000
 you know, megawatt is sort of a symbolic threshold,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.800
 I guess, to get over.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.560
 - Yeah, only lasted two years because it got blown over.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:58.400
 - Yes.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.240
 - Did it.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.080
 - Which I think is sometimes a problem with wind turbines,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:01.920
 isn't it?

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:02.760
 - Occasionally, yeah.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.160
 You do need to build them to withstand that.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.400
 - I know in Denmark, if it gets too windy, they turn off.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.240
 - That's true.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.800
 That kind of, yeah, if it's sort of very stormy conditions.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.840
 - But this guy was like, he was really famous in his time,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:14.840
 wasn't he?

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:18.000
 I read that he went to, he did a lecture at NASA

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.520
 about wind power, and then he signed autographs

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.600
 for 45 minutes afterwards.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.440
 - Really?

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.280
 - That's because they all thought he was going to be like

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.680
 the Edison of wind power, and they thought wind power

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.400
 is going to be the next big thing, so we're going to get

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.240
 his thoughts back.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:31.080
 - Wow.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:31.920
 - Amazing.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.360
 I should say how important this was, because a megawatt,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.920
 it was 10 times bigger than the biggest wind mill

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.120
 that had ever been produced in terms of the electricity

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:41.200
 it could generate.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.040
 - Right.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.560
 - And in fact, most people had wind mills at the time,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.000
 they were about 1,000th of this scale in generation.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.000
 - Like those little ones that you stick in your garden

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.000
 on a little pole.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:50.840
 - Exactly.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.000
 Yeah, farmers had them.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.680
 - Yeah, if you're grinding corn and for the boring,

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.960
 even now to like the Midwest of America,

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.480
 and like in old movies and stuff,

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:01.120
 you see them, they're like bits of metal

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.280
 with tiny windmills on.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.280
 - Yeah, they're sort of like ugly versions

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.360
 of European windmills, aren't they?

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.920
 They're like, disproportionate with a tiny head

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.640
 and a massive body.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.120
 - Yeah, I guess, but the thing is like,

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.800
 because they were pioneers going out west,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.560
 they had to make do with what they had.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.280
 They couldn't just get loads and loads of planks

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.360
 and make an enormous, beautiful wind mill.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.280
 They had to just make it as minimalistic as possible.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:25.120
 But I think it's quite ironic that kind of

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:26.880
 America was built on those windmills,

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.360
 but now the right wing in America just hates wind farms.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:31.720
 - That's true, that's true.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.640
 And after Putnam built this, it took, so this was '41,

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.480
 it took until really the 1970s for wind to take off

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.680
 in a big way, which was partly thanks to the oil crisis,

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.640
 two oil crises in the '70s when there was a, you know,

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.800
 cutoff of fuel from the Middle East.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:47.800
 - Yeah.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.240
 - Those days are gone.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.040
 - Yeah, it was all, so we don't need to worry about that.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.040
 - I just wanted to ask the question on everyone's lips,

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.400
 the most important question about this.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.960
 Who's Grandpa?

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:57.800
 - Well.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.920
 (laughing)

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:01.680
 - And can we be introduced?

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:02.520
 - No.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.240
 (laughing)

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.760
 - I don't think I found why it was called Grandpa's knob.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:07.600
 - I did look.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.920
 - No, I don't think we, I don't think anyone knows

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.680
 unless someone around here has found it.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.400
 - No. - But there is next to it

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.560
 another place called Biddy knob.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.400
 And it seems like maybe Grandpa, because Biddy,

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.680
 it's short for Bridget, but I was also used in America

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.840
 in those days to mean like an old lady.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.320
 So it might have been the Grandpa knob and Biddy knob

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.800
 together made a couple and they might have been named

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.320
 after two people from the area.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:32.440
 - Hey Andy, is there, I think the other main question

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.720
 that listeners of our show wanna know,

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.200
 is there a society to do with wind turbines

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.720
 and windmills and have you joined?

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.440
 - I haven't found one yet, but I don't think

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.200
 there's a Grandpa's knob preservation society

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.240
 because it got, as you said James,

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.920
 it got blown over and damaged in a storm.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.280
 And then a few years ago someone proposed

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.440
 putting up a modern turbine, kind of the same size

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.840
 as a tribute to this amazing engineering first.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.760
 And someone else said, what if it vibrates

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.400
 and so cancel the plan?

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.280
 - Okay.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.960
 - So they've got, but they have got a phone mask there now.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.480
 So you know, that's.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:06.320
 - That's something, isn't it?

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:07.480
 - Progressive source.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.720
 - One more thing on Palmer Putnam,

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.200
 he also designed amphibious landing craft used

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.760
 during the Normandy landings.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:15.960
 - Wow, oh my God.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.320
 - Yeah, he's a big deal.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:19.880
 And later in life, he got into gardening

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:22.400
 and he always planted vegetables next to each other

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.440
 that he knew would grow to the exact same height.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:26.280
 - What?

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.120
 - One of those inventors is more useful than the other,

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:29.960
 I'd say.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:31.800
 - And so they're really big now, aren't they?

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.600
 Are they even bigger than Grandpa's knob?

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:34.840
 - Yeah, yeah, away bigger.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:35.680
 - Wind turbines.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.160
 - Wind turbines, yes.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.320
 And in fact, the biggest wind turbine in the world

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:41.720
 is currently being built in Germany.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:43.680
 It's gonna be 360 meters high,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.200
 which is 100 stories high, which is quite cool.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.960
 And difficult because cranes can only get up to about half

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.120
 that height, so you build half of it.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.640
 They are currently building half of it with cranes.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:59.120
 And then the other half is just telescoped on the inside.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:00.360
 So you get to halfway up.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.200
 - No.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.800
 - And then you pull the rest out like a telescope.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.600
 Yeah, I don't know if it's as simple as that,

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:07.400
 a construction rise, but essentially it.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:08.880
 The other half is inside.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:09.880
 - So cool.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:10.720
 - Clever.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.160
 - I've been reading about windmills, old school.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:14.000
 - Oh yeah.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.600
 - Fascinating, fascinating history.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.600
 It didn't realize there was a code of the windmills.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.600
 So you would do social announcements via your windmill.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:22.440
 - No.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.480
 - Yeah, so I printed out this thing.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.560
 So you can probably have a go at guessing, but okay.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.800
 So you never used to leave your windmill with the,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.080
 so if you had four sails, you would never leave it

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.200
 with the vertical right up.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:36.240
 - Can I say why?

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.080
 - Yes.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.720
 - Because the vertical one going down would stop

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:40.040
 you getting out the door.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:42.640
 (laughing)

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:46.360
 - No, I mean, probably correct.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:48.800
 But what it really was was back in the day,

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.800
 it was lightning bolts.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:51.920
 You would have the highest thing.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:53.200
 And so we're attracting lightning bolts.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.440
 You can leave it there like that now

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.800
 because they have rods that attract the lightning

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:58.760
 in its place.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.200
 But that used to be a way that people,

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.720
 if they saw that the windmill sail was up like that,

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:04.840
 it meant, okay, they're not at home,

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:05.680
 but they'll be back.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.880
 'Cause no fool would leave their sail fully vertical.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:09.720
 - Oh no.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:10.880
 - Yeah, so if you knew that we could live fast.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:12.400
 - Back in five minutes, exactly.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.480
 - And they still use that today for morning.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.080
 So if someone dies, like when the queen died,

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.560
 then all Dutch windmills were in that position, right?

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.240
 It's like what's having flags in our mask.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:25.480
 Yeah, all windmills go to the morning windmill position,

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:27.080
 which is like the crucifix position

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.400
 as opposed to the X marks, the spot position.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:30.240
 - That is amazing.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:31.080
 - Yeah, it's cool.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:31.920
 - And incorrect.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:33.360
 So what you would do is,

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.680
 (laughing)

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:36.520
 Ah.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:40.360
 - It was more of a diagonal direction that you would have it.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.720
 That would be the morning to stress that there was a death.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:43.560
 Yeah, that's a good question.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:44.400
 - No, no.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.120
 - He's absolutely right.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:46.960
 - He's absolutely right.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:47.840
 I was just testing you, Dan.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:49.720
 And you do, you have learned the windmill language

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:50.560
 correctly, well done.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:51.400
 - Thank you.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.240
 And then if you were 12 o'clock and you went to say,

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:55.040
 like you're on your way to one o'clock,

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:58.720
 it's like half 12, the small hands on its way,

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:00.120
 that is celebration.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.520
 We've had a baby.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:03.560
 And so you would see the windmill and go,

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:05.760
 oh my god, they've had another baby.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.360
 - But when it's actually working,

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:08.960
 do you think, baby morning?

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:10.680
 - Baby morning, baby morning.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.440
 (laughing)

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:15.040
 - Picking your moment when to go into the house

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:17.160
 or the door and not be decapitated.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.320
 But yeah, so it is not cool.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:19.800
 It had its own language as well.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:20.640
 - Very cool.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:21.480
 And they go backwards.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:22.320
 - Do they?

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:24.760
 - Yeah, Dutch windmills go round the wrong way.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:28.760
 Or as they would see the right way, counter-clockwise.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:30.720
 Whereas all windmills that are built in the modern day

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:32.160
 go round clockwise.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:33.480
 - We know why.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:36.800
 - Well, it's kind of arbitrary and not a good thing.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.720
 So it's basically because in the '70s when everyone was getting

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:41.360
 the big windmill contracts,

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.560
 there was an engineer called Leif Nielsen, a Danish engineer,

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.560
 and he had a brother working at a rival windmill company.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:50.200
 And his brother was going for the standard counter-clockwise

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.280
 direction, and he didn't like his brother much.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:56.160
 So said, okay, I'm going to make my turbines go round clockwise.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:57.480
 And then he got all the contracts

00:08:57.480 --> 00:08:59.720
 and that's why all windmills go round clockwise today.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:00.560
 - You're joking.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:02.120
 - This is like Aldi Little.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:03.480
 - It's all over again.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:04.320
 - Yeah, yeah.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:06.080
 And it's actually the worst way for them to go round

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:06.920
 in the Northern Hemisphere.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:08.400
 - It's sick of how it's going to be worse.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:12.160
 - There was a study done which showed it actually can generate

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.600
 30% more energy if they go backwards slash anti-clockwise

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:19.960
 because of the Coriolis effects in the Northern Hemisphere,

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:23.200
 which sends the little whirlwinds of air

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.720
 that the turbines generate in the opposite direction,

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:26.960
 it generates more energy.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:29.280
 - Why don't they just put them the other way around?

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:30.880
 - We won't only spin them the other way round.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:32.320
 - No, no, just put them in the facing the other way

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.920
 and then they'll go backwards, right?

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:35.760
 I'll put you in touch.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.960
 - How do I have to do all work for the internet?

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:42.200
 - I read that also that a windmill can never give you

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:47.200
 more than 1627s of the energy of the wind.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.720
 And this is something called Betz's limit.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:52.440
 And I'd love to tell you more about it,

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:55.000
 but literally someone senses that fact two minutes

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:56.160
 before we came on stage.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:58.400
 So I can really have chance to read up on it.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.280
 But I think it's because basically the energy of the wind

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:04.240
 has to go through the sails.

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:06.600
 So if it got 100% of the energy onto it,

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.720
