 OK, it is time for fact number two.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:40.520
 And that is my fact.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.560
 My fact this week is that when he was a child,

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:47.480
 notable Royal Institution lecturer, Humphrey Davy's favorite

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:50.600
 hobby was to lock himself in his room

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:54.200
 and give hour-long talks to a bunch of empty chairs.

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:57.280
 This is Humphrey Davy, who is one of the most spectacular names

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:59.440
 ever to have walked into this building.

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:01.640
 An absolute juggernaut of science.

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:03.160
 We're going to talk about him in a second.

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:06.520
 But one of the things he was notable for outside of his

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:09.200
 discoveries was he was an astonishing lecturer.

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:11.040
 He delivered the most amazing lectures.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:15.120
 And it turns out not only did he have an amazing love of science

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:17.560
 from childhood, but he also just--

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:20.000
 he loved having a crowd of people listen to him

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.080
 and he didn't even need the crowd.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:24.360
 He just needed the idea of a crowd.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.160
 So when he was a child, he used to go out

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:29.240
 and he used to gather kids at the local market

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:31.440
 and he would just rant at them based on anything

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:33.680
 that he found interesting, giving them--

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:34.680
 Yeah, we--

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:37.280
 And then back at home, he would set up those chairs.

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:37.800
 Yeah.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:39.160
 The same children to gather at the market

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:41.760
 will you scream about electricity to--

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:42.760
 It sounds like he's a child too.

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:44.000
 He's a child too.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.120
 OK, no, that is better.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.800
 Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:51.280
 But he was a really-- he was a science-minded child

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:53.240
 and he was always in chemicals.

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:55.200
 He was obsessed with them and he used to do tricks

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:56.040
 on his family.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:59.720
 So for his sisters, he used to take chemicals

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:02.400
 that would be floss-rescent and at night

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.680
 when they turned the-- what, when it got dark,

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:07.040
 suddenly these monsters were on their bedroom wall

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:08.560
 that he painted on with it.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:11.520
 Yeah, so he played lots of tricks with the chemicals

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:12.720
 even as a child.

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:13.640
 That's cool.

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.920
 And then he kind of went into poetry, didn't he?

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:18.160
 He really loved poetry when he was a young man.

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:19.000
 Yeah.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.960
 And he even had some poems published by Robert Southe.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:24.440
 Southe, south-eastern.

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:26.000
 But then what seems to have happened

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.840
 because he's from Cornwall, there was a big shipwreck.

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:31.320
 And on that shipwreck, there was loads of instruments

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:33.480
 from French surgeons and stuff.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.360
 And then he managed to get all of these instruments

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:37.080
 and then started doing loads of experiments

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:38.600
 and then got into science afterwards.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:40.560
 But he was into science, but this is where he started

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:41.560
 to do all of these things.

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:42.720
 Gosh, that's very romantic.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:44.400
 Because he was from-- was it Penn's Ants, I think?

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:46.600
 It's a very proud son of Cornwall and all this.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:47.960
 So he went to Bristol, first of all,

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:48.960
 to start his career.

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:51.440
 And this was the really first, really exciting thing.

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:53.440
 At the age of 20, he was in Bristol

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:56.280
 working for someone called Thomas Bedot, Bedos.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:59.480
 And he was working on Laughing Gas.

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:02.000
 And he was just the sort of young man in the room

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:03.960
 who was, to a certain extent, a guinea pig.

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:05.200
 He was really interested in science.

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:05.800
 But--

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:07.400
 That is the best job ever, though, isn't it?

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:09.800
 When all your friends are interning at the dad's printing

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:12.400
 company, you're the guinea pig for Laughing Gas?

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:13.840
 Yeah.

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:15.480
 And he really got into it.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:19.760
 I mean, he took a lot of Laughing Gas for a long time.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:21.760
 And that was the time when he knew all these poets.

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:22.560
 So he knew Southe.

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:23.920
 He knew Samuel Taylor Colourish.

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:25.000
 This is all in Bristol.

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:29.360
 He was friends with Roger of the Soros fame.

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:30.160
 Yeah.

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:30.960
 I know.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:31.800
 Do you know what?

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:34.040
 I read Roger's account of taking Laughing Gas.

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:35.200
 Because what Henry David did was, he said,

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:37.600
 he'd even come to all these Laughing Gas parties I'm throwing.

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:41.160
 But you have to write down notes of exactly how you found it.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.760
 And so he compiled this book of all their notes.

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:45.400
 And Roger--

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:46.560
 It sounds dreadful.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.480
 It sounds like a load of people's anecdotes about getting

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:49.520
 stoned, as in it sounds like--

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:50.200
 Oh, it's brilliant.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:51.040
 I've read it.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:52.040
 Yeah.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:53.880
 Oh, well, maybe it's more at my street than yours,

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:55.280
 because it is just what you described.

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:56.120
 Yeah, OK.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:58.880
 I suppose it's not like your mates telling you.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:01.880
 It's like the greatest poets of the 90th century.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:02.400
 Yeah.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.040
 Still think drug experiences and dreams

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:06.240
 are the two things that are terrific in your own head

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:07.000
 and then kind of--

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:08.720
 No, it's the original--

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:11.600
 He was Davies, the original Aldous Huxley

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:12.480
 doors a perception.

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:13.280
 That's what he was doing.

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:15.520
 He was trying to show that your brain went

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:17.160
 into a different state of consciousness

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:18.440
 when you were on these things.

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:19.840
 And what you were opening yourself up to,

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:20.720
 that's the bit I can see.

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:23.120
 To be honest, that is the most tedious thing

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:24.640
 about people who've taken drugs as well.

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:26.560
 They talk about the Aldous States of Consciousness.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:27.720
 I just want to hear that they sort of

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:30.840
 shat themselves in a corner at their grandma's house.

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:33.480
 If it's that violent, that's true, yeah.

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:36.680
 Which is, and rosary of the Soros fame, his account--

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:37.800
 well, here's what it is.

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:40.120
 He said, I was incapable of speaking,

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:42.120
 lost all consciousness of where I was.

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:44.960
 My whole frame was violently agitated.

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:47.360
 I panted violently, and my heart seemed

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:51.080
 to palbitate in every artery to throb with violence

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:54.040
 of the Soros fame.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:58.160
 He said the word violent about 19 times in his account.

00:16:58.160 --> 00:16:59.240
 He hated it.

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:00.080
 Most of them loved it.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:02.240
 He said, I cannot remember that I experienced the least

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:03.920
 pleasure from these sensations.

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:04.520
 Really?

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:05.040
 Yeah.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:06.560
 His colrich loved it.

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:08.720
 Davey really loved it.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:11.440
 They got to a point where the party died down,

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:12.800
 and no one wanted to do it anymore,

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:14.360
 but he just kept doing it.

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:18.120
 And he went around with a sort of green silk bag

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:20.200
 that he would just fill up with the stuff,

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:21.600
 and he would go for long walks.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:23.840
 And he would-- he would do it to the point where he'd pass out,

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:28.160
 wake up two hours later, not realizing how much he had done,

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:30.280
 and then get back on the horse and do it some more.

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:31.080
 He'd-- yeah.

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:32.960
 And once got that friend from Uni, haven't they?

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:35.320
 Sort of 10 years later, oh, you're still--

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:36.320
 Yeah.

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:39.320
 OK.

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:40.120
 He did move on.

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:43.520
 He moved to London, and he came to the Royal Institution

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:46.200
 and invented the minor safety lamp on the rest of his history.

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:46.960
 Yeah.

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:49.880
 But yeah, he did loads of lectures in this very room, didn't he?

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:53.480
 And they were so popular that the traffic around here

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:56.280
 got really, really busy, and they made a one-way street

00:17:56.280 --> 00:17:57.680
 so that people could get in and out,

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:00.160
 and it was the first one-way street in the whole England.

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:02.920
 So yeah, the Royal Institution is on Album Alstree, which

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:05.720
 is just outside us, and so many people

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:08.400
 were coming horse and carriage and walking and crowding

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:11.960
 to see Davey speak, that they had to change the whole system

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:14.760
 so that they could just stop London coming to a standstill

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:15.360
 of the people--

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:17.360
 Which is actually a huge fact when you're cycling here

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:18.760
 on the way to a show and you're late.

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:19.600
 So good to know.

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:23.200
 I have him to blame for my near death experience today.

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:26.280
 He also-- he discovered five elements,

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:29.640
 which he showed at the Royal Institution for the first time.

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:31.040
 So calcium.

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:31.680
 Biggie.

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:33.040
 Biggie.

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:35.040
 Strontium.

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:36.680
 Yes, it's like B side, yeah.

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:37.400
 Yeah.

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:38.200
 Barium.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:38.720
 Barium.

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:40.720
 Barium.

00:18:40.720 --> 00:18:41.520
 OK.

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:43.760
 Unless it was Daveed after a guy called Barry.

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:45.440
 Yeah.

