 Boron.

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:47.720
 Yeah?

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:48.800
 Magnesium.

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:49.600
 Biggie.

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:50.600
 Biggie.

00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:51.600
 Yeah, yeah.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:52.440
 These are huge.

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:55.360
 And but he said his greatest discovery of all

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:56.760
 was not a chemical.

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:58.920
 It was not any of the experiments.

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:01.720
 It was a little student called Michael Faraday.

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:03.280
 And Michael Faraday is also--

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:05.160
 Oh, good noise.

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:06.000
 Yeah.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:09.520
 Not many rooms you'd get that noise in when you drop Faraday's

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:10.200
 name.

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:12.800
 Faraday had come to a bunch of lectures

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:14.840
 that he'd seen Davey do when he was younger.

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:18.320
 And he scribbled down this big booklet of ideas and so on.

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:19.920
 And he sent it to Davey.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:23.520
 And Davey saw so much potential that he brought him in.

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:27.520
 But Faraday is also one of the great giants of this room.

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:30.080
 Didn't Davey have a big accident or something?

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:33.480
 He was working with nitrogen trichloride, which

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:37.080
 is a huge explosive, and it exploded in his face.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:39.480
 And he went partially blind for quite a long time.

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:40.920
 And he needed a helper.

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:43.320
 And that's when he brought Faraday in to help him.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:46.680
 So this one big accident was what brought them together,

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:49.160
 to work together, what really gave us Faraday.

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:51.400
 He was reckless, I would say.

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:54.440
 He used to shut himself in a box with the gases

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.000
 he was inhaling to make sure he got the most intense high

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:57.520
 possible.

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:00.360
 And there was one time when he sent all of his servants away,

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:03.120
 because he just wanted to, if you're having a big high,

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:05.000
 you want to enjoy it by yourself alone.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:07.840
 And one of his servants didn't get that I'm told.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:10.880
 One of his servants didn't get the memo,

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:12.960
 didn't realize that they'd all been dismissed.

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:16.200
 And so he came to see if Humphrey wanted a cigar or something.

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.920
 Came and found him in this shut box

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:21.320
 that Humphrey built for himself, specifically, completely

00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:24.040
 unconscious, asphyxiaating, and dragged him out.

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:26.720
 And he would have died had this servant not rescued him.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:28.360
 So he did take self-experimentation

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:31.880
 to someone say quite stupid limits.

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:33.800
 But can we say the thing about the safety lamp

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:35.920
 where he saved a lot of lives?

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:36.520
 This is incredible.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:39.000
 So miners were dying by the dozen,

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:40.920
 and in fact, many more than that, due to something

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:44.280
 called fire damp, which we would call methane.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:47.840
 We have these pockets of methane underground that catch fire.

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:50.080
 And the only way of lighting your mine underground

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:51.080
 is with candles.

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:52.640
 So there's a huge problem.

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:56.520
 And I think it was about two weeks on the problem

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:58.000
 before designing a lamp.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:00.240
 And there was a bit of debate about whether there

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:01.080
 was a different kind of lamp.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:04.880
 Stevenson of Rocket fame came up with another version of one.

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:07.280
 But this-- from what it looks like, this was definitely

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:08.560
 was Davies invention.

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:11.600
 And it's basically just to find sort of cylinder of gauze

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:12.800
 around the light that you're using.

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:14.520
 And kind of in closes the light, doesn't it?

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:17.240
 Yeah, you still get the light, but you don't get the methane

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:19.440
 exploding, even if you're in a dangerous area.

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:23.400
 And he refused to patent it, which was very exciting.

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:25.680
 And I think for over a century, coal miners

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:28.640
 carried these things and did not die due to that--

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:29.560
 at least due to methane.

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:31.880
 They died plenty of other things.

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:34.720
 And what this did was it made deeper

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:37.800
 and bigger seams of coal accessible.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:39.920
 And a lot of the industrial revolution

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:44.200
 was powered by miners being able to access lots more coal.

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:47.240
 So that huge change in the world was at least partly thanks

00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:48.440
 to Davies' lamp.

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:52.120
 But did any of you think that it's quite basic?

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:55.240
 It's literally all he does is put--

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:57.280
 is like you put a sieve over a candle.

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:58.280
 That's all it is.

00:21:58.280 --> 00:21:59.400
 Miners are going into mines.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:01.280
 Candles are causing explosions.

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:03.320
 And Davies says, you know what, if you put a sieve over this,

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:06.400
 the metal gauze, because it's metal, it absorbs all the heat.

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:09.040
 So you don't have the heat for that big explosion.

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:09.640
 And that's it.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:10.640
 Put a sieve over it.

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:12.440
 So the best ideas do seem simple.

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:14.360
 Once they've been discovered, right?

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.760
 I just reckon I would have come up with that in my kitchen

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:17.440
 at some point.

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:18.560
 I'm not.

00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:20.160
 Are you sure?

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:21.800
 I can't be 100% but--

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:23.840
 [LAUGHTER]

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:26.840
 I just think, yeah, combining it with any utensil in your house,

00:22:26.840 --> 00:22:28.440
 I wouldn't think of the sieve, first of all.

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:30.440
 Have we invented the sieve at this point?

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:31.560
 Yeah, the sieve wasn't--

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:32.400
 Ooh.

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:33.400
 Ooh.

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:33.920
 Oh my god.

