 But no, he went to the Louvre, and then he

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:00.600
 wrote back to his friend, Soniais, all right?

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:02.600
 It's just full of stuff that Napoleon stole, isn't it?

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:03.800
 All right, yeah.

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:07.040
 He went to-- there was an anti-British play on at the time,

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:09.080
 and he went and sort of sat in the back and watched

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:11.240
 to see what they were saying about us.

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:14.600
 And the main thing was, there's another chemist called La Voixier,

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:16.480
 and La Voixier had done all this stuff

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:19.280
 about the science of heat.

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:22.560
 And Davy disagreed with La Voixier's theories.

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:24.320
 And in the end, he managed to prove it.

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:26.000
 And when he proved it, in his paper,

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:28.280
 he was just all about how the French were.

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:32.080
 So dogmatic about their ideas and how we finally proven them wrong.

00:25:32.080 --> 00:25:34.280
 And so, yeah, he was just like a real--

00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:36.280
 he was like the Andrew Hunter Murray of his day.

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:37.280
 Wow.

